        <div id="why-expensive" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Why Is Some Protein Powder So Expensive?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The four things that push a powder above $2.50 per 25 g are the protein source, the size of the bag, the sales channel and the marketing story. Testing and certification add surprisingly little: the NSF Certified for Sport powders in our database range from $1.25 to $3.97 per 25 g, about the same spread as uncertified ones.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The protein source</h3>

          <p>
            Isolate, hydrolysate, grass-fed claims, egg white, beef and bone broth all cost more to make or buy than standard whey concentrate. Some of that premium buys something real (less lactose with isolate, a dairy-free animal protein with egg or beef), and some of it buys a label word. Grass-fed whey, for example, has a similar amino acid profile to conventional whey; you pay for the farming standard, not for more muscle-building value.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Small bags and subscriptions</h3>

          <p>
            Many direct-to-consumer brands sell 15 to 30 servings in a stand-up bag and push a monthly subscription. Bags of that size cost more per gram than a 5 lb tub, and the brands set their list price high enough that the subscription discount still leaves them a healthy margin. Of the 20 powders that cost $3 or more per 25 g in our database, more than half are small tubs or 15-to-30-serving bags sold mainly through the brand itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where it is sold</h3>

          <p>
            The same product costs different amounts in different places. Gold Standard 100% Whey is $79.99 for 3.96 lb at Target in our database and $124.99 for 5.05 lb at The Vitamin Shoppe, which is $1.44 and $1.76 per 25 g respectively before either store's promotions. Specialty stores, gyms and pharmacies usually charge more for name brands than warehouse clubs, supercenters and Amazon; our store guides for <a href="/best-walmart-protein-powder/">Walmart</a>, <a href="/best-costco-protein-powder/">Costco</a> and <a href="/best-aldi-protein-powder/">Aldi</a> show the cheapest tubs at each.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The story</h3>

          <p>
            Celebrity founders, superfood blends, collagen add-ins and adaptogens all add cost without adding protein. That is not a reason never to buy them, but it is a reason to price the protein separately from the extras and ask whether you would pay the difference for the extras alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-food" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Protein Powder Cheaper Than Food?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Budget protein powder is about as cheap per gram of protein as the cheapest whole foods, and premium powder is more expensive than most of them. At the database median of $2.26 per 25 g, powder costs more than eggs, chicken breast, dry beans or Greek yogurt bought on sale. At $1.00 to $1.50 per 25 g, it is competitive with all of them and far more convenient.
          </p>

          <p>
            Food prices vary too much by region and week for us to quote one, so here is the arithmetic instead. A dozen large eggs holds about 72 g of protein, so at $4.00 a dozen eggs cost about $1.39 per 25 g; at $3.00 a dozen, about $1.04. Raw chicken breast is roughly 23 g of protein per 100 g, so a pound holds about 104 g of protein; at $4.00 a pound that is about $0.96 per 25 g. A 32 oz tub of plain nonfat Greek yogurt holds roughly 80 to 90 g of protein; at $6.00 that is about $1.75 per 25 g.
          </p>

