        <div id="pay-less" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Can You Pay Less for Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The biggest savings come from buying the largest tub of a powder you already know you like, and from buying it during a sale that takes 20 to 30 percent off. Those two habits alone usually move a powder from the premium band into the normal band. The rest are smaller wins that add up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy bulk once you know the flavor. A 5 lb tub usually costs 15 to 30 percent less per gram than a 2 lb tub of the same product. Naked prices its 5 lb whey at about $28 a pound against $38 for the 1 lb bag, and Isopure Zero Carb at The Vitamin Shoppe drops from about $2.93 an ounce in the 14 oz tub to $2.37 in the 3.96 lb tub. Do not buy 5 lb of a flavor you have never tried; a tub you throw away is the most expensive protein there is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the store-brand or retailer exclusive where one exists. Kirkland at Costco, Elevation at Aldi, Equate at Walmart, Meijer's own whey and BodyTech at The Vitamin Shoppe are all cheaper per gram than the name brands on the same shelf. Our <a href="/best-vitamin-shoppe-protein-powder/">Vitamin Shoppe protein powder guide</a> and <a href="/best-meijer-protein-powder/">Meijer guide</a> work through the house brands label by label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Time big purchases for sale events. Black Friday and Cyber Monday, Amazon's July and October deal events, January and brand anniversary sales are when most powders hit their lowest prices of the year. Our <a href="/black-friday-protein-powder-deals/">Black Friday protein powder deals guide</a> lists the price per 25 g that counts as a real deal for each type, so you can tell a discount from a reset list price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Subscribe, but watch the price. Amazon Subscribe and Save and most brand subscriptions take 5 to 15 percent off, but you pay the price on the day the order ships, not the day you subscribed. Skip a month if the price has crept up. And buy unflavored if you blend shakes with fruit or coffee anyway: unflavored tubs are often the cheapest size in a range and you avoid paying for sweetener and flavoring you would cover up.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The rule of thumb</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Under $1.50 per 25 g is cheap. Over $3.00 is a premium you should be able to name.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you cannot say what the extra money buys (lactose-free isolate, a sport certification you need, a dairy-free protein, organic sourcing), buy the cheaper powder.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tub prices instead of cost per 25 g</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $20 tub and a $60 tub can be equally good value, or the cheap one can be the rip-off. Tub price ignores servings and protein per scoop. Divide the price by servings, then by grams of protein per serving, and multiply by 25 before deciding which powder is cheaper.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a mass gainer as cheap protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mass gainers look cheap per pound because most of each pound is carbohydrate. Serious Mass costs $3.62 per 25 g of protein, more than most whey isolates. Buy a gainer only if you need the calories, and buy whey if you only need protein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the trial size every time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small tubs and 15-serving bags cost the most per gram in almost every range. Once you know you like a flavor, the 5 lb tub of the same product typically costs 15 to 30 percent less per gram, and it keeps for well over a year sealed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming expensive means tested</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Price and certification are barely related. NSF Certified for Sport powders in our database run from $1.25 to $3.97 per 25 g. If testing matters to you, look up the product on the NSF or Informed Sport registry rather than paying more and hoping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a strike-through price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Retailers and brands sometimes raise a list price before a sale, so a 40 percent discount can land on the usual price. Compare the sale price with the cost per 25 g tables on this page, or a price-history tool, before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much does protein powder cost on average?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Across the 104 priced powders in our label database in November 2026, the median tub cost $58.99, the median serving cost $2.11 and the median cost per 25 g of protein was $2.26. Half of all tubs fell between about $45 and $75. Sale prices, store brands and bulk sizes all come in below these figures, while small premium bags come in well above them.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is a good price per serving for protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Under about $1.50 a serving is good for a 24-25 g serving of whey or pea protein, and under $1.00 is excellent. Per-serving prices only compare fairly when the servings hold the same protein, so check the protein per scoop too. A 50 g serving at $2.50 is cheaper protein than a 20 g serving at $1.80.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why is whey isolate more expensive than whey concentrate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isolate is filtered further to remove most of the lactose, fat and carbohydrate, so it takes more milk to make each kilo. In our database the median isolate costs $2.43 per 25 g against $1.91 for concentrate. The extra cost is worth it if lactose bothers you or you are counting calories tightly, and mostly wasted if neither applies.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is plant protein powder cheaper than whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Plain pea protein is the cheapest protein we track: Naked Pea at $0.71 per 25 g and NOW Sports Pea at $0.91. Flavored organic plant blends are often the most expensive, with a median of $2.51 per 25 g and some above $4. Whether plant is cheaper depends almost entirely on whether you buy plain pea or a branded blend.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How long does a tub of protein powder last?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  A 2 lb tub usually holds 25 to 30 servings, so about a month at one shake a day or two weeks at two. A 5 lb tub holds 65 to 80 servings, two to two and a half months at one shake a day. Sealed tubs typically carry a best-by date 18 to 24 months out; see our guide to whether protein powder expires.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is it cheaper to buy protein powder online or in store?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Usually online, but not always. Amazon, Target.com and Walmart.com tend to beat specialty stores and pharmacies on name brands, while store brands such as Kirkland, Elevation and Equate are only sold in their own stores and are often the cheapest per gram of all. Compare cost per 25 g, not shelf price, wherever you buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>When is protein powder cheapest?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Black Friday through Cyber Monday is usually the cheapest window of the year, followed by Amazon's July and October deal events and January sales. A good sale takes 20 to 30 percent off the list price. Check that the sale price per 25 g is below the usual price for that type before buying, since some list prices are raised ahead of the event.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

